When Grignard reagents react with carbon dioxide (CO₂), the primary product is a carboxylic acid. This occurs because the Grignard reagent acts as a nucleophile, attacking the electrophilic carbon of CO₂, forming a carbonyl anion intermediate. This intermediate is then protonated during the workup (usually with an acid) to yield the carboxylic acid. 

