","When \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) gas is added to a solution containing \( \text{Mn}^{2+}, \text{Ni}^{2+}, \text{Cu}^{2+}, \) and \( \text{Hg}^{2+} \) ions in an acidified aqueous solution, the presence of \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) acts as a sulfide source (\( \text{H}_2\text{S} \rightarrow \text{HS}^- + \text{H}^+ \)). The presence of \( \text{H}^+ \) (acidic solution) is important because it prevents \( \text{S}^{2-} \) ions from forming, which would compete with \( \text{HS}^- \) and alter the solubility behavior.

The correct order of solubility of metal sulfides is:
\[ \text{HgS} < \text{CuS} < \text{MnS} < \text{NiS} \]
This means that \( \text{HgS} \) is the least soluble, followed by \( \text{CuS} \), \( \text{MnS} \), and \( \text{NiS} \).

The solubility product constants (\( K_{sp} \)) for the metal sulfides are:
