Fe$^{3+}(aq) + 6CN^-(aq) \rightleftharpoons \text{FeCN}_6^{4-}(aq)$

The equilibrium constant \( K_{eq} = \frac{[\text{FeCN}_6^{4-}]}{[\text{Fe}^{3+}][\text{CN}^-]^6} = 1.2 \times 10^{20} \).

Initial concentrations:
- \([\text{Fe}^{3+}] = 1.0 \, \text{M}\)
- \([\text{CN}^-] = 0 \, \text{M}\) (since no KCN was added initially)
- \([\text{FeCN}_6^{4-}] = 0 \, \text{M}\)

When 0.25 moles of KCN is added to the solution, the concentration of CN\(^-\) will increase. Since the volume of the solution is not given, we assume the volume is 1 L. Therefore, the concentration of CN\(^-\) added is \(\frac{0.25 \, \text{moles}}{1 \, \text{L}} = 0.25 \, \text{M}\).

Now, we can set up an ICE (Initial, Change, Equilibrium) table:

|| \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\) | \(\text{CN}^-\) | \(\text{FeCN}_6^{4-}\) |
---|-------------|---------|---------|
| **Initial (I)** | 1.0 | 0.25 | 0 |
| **Change (C)** | -x | -6x | +x |
| **Equilibrium (E)** | 1.0 - x | 0.25 - 6x | x |

The equilibrium constant expression is:
\[
K_{eq} = \frac{[\text{FeCN}_6^{4-}]}{[\text{Fe}^{3+}][\text{CN}^-]^6} = \frac{x}{(1.0 - x)(0.25 - 6x)^6} = 1.2 \times 10^{20}
\]

