","To solve this problem, we first determine the distance A has passed when B starts cycling.

A's speed is 10 kmph.
B starts 3 hours after A.
In these 3 days, A passes:
\[
\text{Distance} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time} = 10 \times 3 = 30 \text{ km}
\]

Now, let \( t \) be the time (in hours) B cycles after B stops. During this time, A and B are both moving.
A's speed is 10 kmph, and B's speed is 20 kmph.
The relative speed between B and A is \( 20 - 10 = 10 \) kmph.

For B to cache up with A, B must make up the 30 km head-start A has.
\[
\text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Relative Speed}} = \frac{30}{10} = 3 \text{ hours}
\]
So, B cycles for 3 hours to cache up with A.

The distance B cycles is:
\[
\text{Distance} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time} = 20 \times 3 = 60 \text{ km}
\]
The total time A has been moving is \( 3 + 3 = 6 \) hours.
The total distance A has traveled is:
\[
\text{Distance} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time} = 10 \times 6 = 60 \text{ km}
\]
Both A and B are at the same position when B caches up with A, which is 60 km from the start.

Thus, B caches up with A 60 km from the start.

Final answer: $\boxed{60}$",relevance,"# Task Description
