\]
where:
- \( B(\lambda, T) \) is the intensity of the radiation at wavelength \( \lambda \) and temperature \( T \).
- \( h \) is the Planck constant (\( 6.626 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J s} \)).
- \( c \) is the speed of light in vacuum (\( 3.00 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s} \)).
- \( \lambda \) is the wavelength of the radiation.
- \( k \) is the Boltzmann constant (\( 1.381 \times 10^{-23} \, \text{J/K} \)).
- \( T \) is the temperature of the body (in Kelvin).

This formula describes the spectral radiation intensity, which is the power per unit area per unit solid angle per unit wavelength. 

Thus, the expression for the intensity of thermal radiation of a blackbody in terms of temperature \( T \) and wavelength \( \lambda \) is:
