","To solve this problem, we use the conservation of momentum. The total momentum of the system (bullet + Earth) must be conserved, meaning it remains zero (assuming the system is initially at rest).

The initial momentum is zero:
\[
\text{P}_{\text{initial}} = 0
\]

After the bullet is fired, the momentum of the bullet is \(m_b v_b\), and the momentum of the Earth is \(M v_E\), where \(m_b\) is the mass of the bullet, \(v_b\) is the velocity of the bullet, \(M\) is the mass of the Earth, and \(v_E\) is the velocity of the Earth. The total momentum after firing is:
\[
\text{P}_{\text{final}} = m_b v_b + M v_E = 0
\]

Solving for \(v_E\):
\[
v_E = -\frac{m_b v_b}{M}
\]

Substituting the given values:
\[
v_E = -\frac{(1 \text{ kg})(90.0 \text{ m/s})}{5.97 \times 10^{24} \text{ kg}} = -\frac{90.0}{5.97 \times 10^{24}} \text{ m/s}
\]

Calculating the value:
\[
v_E \approx -1.51 \times 10^{-23} \text{ m/s}
\]

The negative sign indicates that the Earth moves in the direction opposite to the bullet. The magnitude of the recoil velocity is:
\[
|v_E| \approx 1.51 \times 10^{-23} \text{ m/s}
\]

Thus, the approximate recoil velocity of the Earth is \(\boxed{1.51 \times 10^{-23} \text{ m/s}}\).",fluency,"# Task Description
