","The net chemical reaction during the discharging of a lead-acid (lead-sulfur) series cell is:  
\[ \text{Pb} + \text{H}_2\text{SO}_4 \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4 + 2\text{H}^+ \]  

This reaction occurs at the negative (anode) electrode.  








The positive (cathode) electrode reaction is:  
\[ \text{PbO}_2 + \text{H}_2\text{SO}_4 + 2\text{H}^+ \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} \]  

The overall reaction, combining both half-reactions, is:  
\[ \text{Pb} + \text{PbO}_2 + 2\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4 \rightarrow 2\text{PbSO}_4 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} \]  

Thus, the lead and lead dioxide are converted to lead sulfate, and the hydrogen sulfate (HSO4-) ions are consumed, producing water.   The lead sulfate precipitates on both electrodes.  

Final answer: $\boxed{\text{Pb} + \text{PbO}_2 + 2\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4 \rightarrow 2\text{PbSO}_4 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O}}$",ground_truth_similarity,"# Task Description
